<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>1.2. The FTC of line integrals</title>

    <p>
    <alert>
        MTH 401
    </alert>
  </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-conservative-field">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A field <m>\mbf F</m> is <term>conservative</term> if there
                exists some function <m>f</m> for which <m>{F = \nabla f}</m>.
                The function <m>f</m> is called the
                <term>potential function for <m>\mbf F</m></term>.
            </p>

            <p>
                (Note that physicists usually use <m>F=-\nabla f</m>, but it is
                essentially the same.)
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-conservative-FTC">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>FTC for line integrals</term>: if <m>\mbf F</m> is
                conservative and <m>\mbf F = \nabla f</m>, then
                <md>
                    \lineint{C}{F} = f(Q)-f(P)
                </md>
                where <m>C</m> goes from point <m>P</m> to point <m>Q</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <exercise workspace="1.25in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>{\mathbf F} = \nabla f</m> where
                <m>f(x,y) = 3x^2y -
  \sin(x) + e^y</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the coordinates of the field <m>{\mathbf F}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                <m>\ds \mathbf F= \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)=(6xy-\cos x, 3x^2+e^y)</m>
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate <m>\lineint{C_1}{F}</m> where <m>C_1</m> is the
                    upper half of the unit circle oriented in the
                    counter-clockwise direction.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                <md>\lineint{C_1}{F}=\lineint{C_1}{\nabla f} = f(-1,0) - f(1,0)</md>
                <md>
                  = (-\sin(-1)+1)-(-\sin(1)+1) = \sin(1)+1+\sin(1)-1 = 2\sin(1)
                </md>
                This uses <m>\sin(-1)=-\sin(1)</m> since <m>\sin(x)</m> is an odd function, but you do 
                not need to simplify all the way to here.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate <m>\lineint{C_2}{F}</m> where <m>C_2</m> is the
                    straight line segment from <m>(1,0)</m> to <m>(-1,0)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                It's a conservative field so path doesn't matter.  It's still <m>2\sin(1)</m>.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0.1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>{\mathbf F}(x,y) = (2x, 3y)</m>.
                Evaluate
                <md>
                    \lineint{C_1}{F}, ~~~
  \lineint{C_2}{F}
                </md>
                where <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are the curves as shown below (<m>C_1</m> has two pieces!).
            </p>

            <image width="30%" margins="0% 70%">
                <shortdescription>
                    The graph of three line segments with directions, see
                    description below.
                </shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-3-line-integral">
                    <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                        <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,4,4)">
                            <grid-axes spacings="((-1,1,4),(-1,1,4))" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                            <vector v="(-2,0)" tail="(2,0)" stroke="blue" head-location="0.8"/>
                            <vector v="(-2,2)" tail="(2,0)" stroke="blue" head-location="0.8"/>
                            <vector v="(0,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue" head-location="0.8"/>
                            <label anchor="(1.5,0)" alignment="south" offset="(0,-5)">
                                <m>
                                    \displaystyle C_1
                                </m>
                            </label>
                            <label anchor="(1,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(5,5)">
                                <m>
                                    \displaystyle C_2
                                </m>
                            </label>
                        </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>

                <description>
                    <p>
                        Three directional curves are shown, <m>C_1</m> is the
                        straight line from <m>(2,0)</m> to <m>(0,0)</m>,
                        <m>C_2</m> is the line from <m>(2,0)</m> to
                        <m>(0,2)</m>, and the third line goes from <m>(0,0)</m>
                        to <m>(0,2)</m>.
                    </p>
                </description>
            </image>
        </statement>
        <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                If <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative, this problem becomes nicer!  And it is! 
                <md>
                  \mathbf F = \nabla f = (2x,3y)
                </md>
                so <m>f = x^2+\frac32 y^2</m> (plus any constant but we'll choose 0) works!
                <md>
                  \lineint{C_1}{F} = \lineint{C_2}{F} = f(0,2) - f(2,0)=6-4=2.
                </md>
                
              </p>
            </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's look closer at fields and the concept of a conservative field.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Assume <m>C</m> is a closed curve; that is, <m>C</m> has the
                    same initial and final point.
                    If <m>{\mathbf F}</m> is a conservative vector field, what
                    is the line integral <m>\lineint{C}{F}</m>?
                    (Note: we often write such as integral as
                    <m>\olineint{C}{F}</m> to denote the fact that <m>C</m> is
                    closed.) Explain your response.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                If we have a closed curve <m>C</m>, it starts and ends at the same point <m>P</m>. 
                Since <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative, there exists some function <m>f</m> such that 
                <m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m>, so <m>\lineint{C}{F} = f(P)-f(P) = 0</m>. Work over a closed curve is 
                always zero in a conservative field.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that
                    <md>
                        {\mathbf F}(x,y) = \left(-\frac{y}{x^2+y^2},
    \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\right)
                    </md>
                    and <m>C</m> is the unit circle traversed one time in the
                    counter-clockwise direction.
                    Find the line integral <m>\olineint{C}{F}</m>.
                    (You did this last worksheet.)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                See last worksheet and spare yourself busy work.  But it looks like this:
              </p>
              <p>
            Parameterization <md>x = \cos(t), y=\sin(t)</md> (or <m>\vec{r}(t)=(\cos(t),\sin(t))</m>), with
                <m>0\le t \le 2\pi</m>.  Then <m>dx = -\sin(t)dt, dy=\cos(t)dt</m> (or <m>d\vec{r}(t)=(-\sin(t)dt,\cos(t)dt)</m>), 
                but also <md>x^2+y^2 = \cos^2(t)+\sin^2(t)=1</md> so 
                the integral becomes <md>\lineint{C}{F}=\int_{C}\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2}dx + \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}dy </md>
                <md>=
                   \int_0^{2\pi} -\sin t(-\sin t) + \cos t(\cos t) dt 
                </md><md>= \int_0^{2\pi} \sin^2(t) + \cos^2(t) dt
                </md><md>  = \int_0^{2\pi} 1 dt</md><md> = 2\pi</md>
          </p>
          <p>
            Notice it is not zero.
          </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is <m>{\mathbf F}</m> in the previous part conservative?
                    Explain your response.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                Nope!  If it was a conservative field this integral would be 0 since it's a closed curve.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>C</m> is the curve parametrized by
                <m>{\mathbf r}(t) = (t^2, t^3, t-1)</m> where
                <m>1\leq t \leq 2</m>.
                Evaluate the line integral
                <md>
                    \int_C ye^z~dx + xe^z~dy + xye^z~dz.
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                Let's try the conservative trick because that seems much nicer than integration by parts five times. 
                Picking apart the integral which is <m>\lineint{C}{F}</m>, we are looking for <md>\mathbf F =
                  (ye^z, xe^z, xye^z) = \nabla f.
                </md>
                So <m>f(x,y,z)=xye^z</m>!. 
                <md>
                  \lineint{C}{F} = f(t=2) - f(t=1) = f(4,8,1) - f(1,1,0) = 32e - 1
                </md>
                
              </p>
            </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace=".5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which, if either, of the fields below could be conservative?
                Explain your response.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="30% 30%">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A vector field, all vectors point radially away from the
                        origin.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-3-slope1">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x,y) = (x,y)
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <vector-field function="f"/>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A vector field, all arrows points counter-clockwise in a
                        co-centric circles centered around the origin.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-3-slope2">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x,y) = (-y,x)
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <vector-field function="f"/>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
        </statement>
        <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                Think about closed curves, and the work done on them. For the first picture, it 
                seems that any closed curve you could draw would have the work be zero, but the second 
                one you can find a closed curve that is not zero. So the second field cannot be conservative. (Examples
                of closed curves shown below, but there are many you can draw.)
              </p>
              <sidebyside widths="30% 30%">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A vector field, all vectors point radially away from the
                        origin.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-3-slope1-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x,y) = (x,y)
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                
                                <vector-field function="f"/>
                                <arc center="(0,0)" radius="2" stroke="red" arrows="1" range="(20,380)"/>
                                <arc center="(0,0)" radius="4"  stroke="red" range="(295,335)" arrows="1"/>
                                <arc center="(0,0)" radius="3"  stroke="red" range="(295,335)" />
                                <line p1="(3*cos(295 * 2*pi/360), 3*sin(295* 2*pi/360))" p2="(4*cos(295* 2*pi/360),4*sin(295* 2*pi/360))" stroke="red" />
                                <line p1="(3*cos(335* 2*pi/360), 3*sin(335* 2*pi/360))" p2="(4*cos(335* 2*pi/360),4*sin(335* 2*pi/360))" stroke="red"  />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                    
                </image>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A vector field, all arrows points counter-clockwise in a
                        co-centric circles centered around the origin.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-3-slope2-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x,y) = (-y,x)
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <vector-field function="f"/>
                                <arc center="(0,0)" radius="2" stroke="red" range="(20,380)" arrows="1" />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
            </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                From MTH 203 (Calculus III), you may remember that the second
                mixed partials of a reasonable function <m>f(x,y)</m> are equal;
                that is,
                <md>
                    \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x\partial y} =
  \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y\partial x}.
                </md>
                That is, it doesn't matter in which order we compute second
                mixed partials.
            </p>

            <p>
                This gives us a simple test to determine when a field
                <m>{\mathbf
    F}(x,y)</m> is conservative.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>
  {\mathbf F}(x,y) = (F_1(x,y), F_2(x,y))
  </m> is
                    conservative, what relationship must hold between <m>F_1</m>
                    and <m>F_2</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                So we must have <m>\nabla f = (F_1, F_2)</m>, which means <m>{F_1 = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}}</m>
                and <m>{F_2 = \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}}</m>. Since the second mixed partial derivatives are equal, then
                <md>
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial x}\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right) = 
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial x}\left(F_2\right) = 
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial y}\left(F_1\right)=
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial y}\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right) 
                </md>
                so in other words if <m>\mathbf F=(F_1,F_2)</m> is a conservative field, we must have 
                <md>
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial x}\left(F_2\right) = 
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial y}\left(F_1\right)
                </md>
                
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Verify that this relationship holds for the field in
                    problems 1 and 2.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                Problem 1: <m>\ds \mathbf F= (6xy-\cos x, 3x^2+e^y)</m>, 
                <md>
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial x}\left(F_2\right) = 6x = \frac{\partial }{\partial y}\left(F_1\right)
                </md>.
                Check.
              </p>
              <p>
                Problem 2: <m>\ds \mathbf F= (2x,3y)</m>, 
                <md>
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial x}\left(F_2\right) = 0 = \frac{\partial }{\partial y}\left(F_1\right)
                </md>.
                Check.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace=".7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Does this relationship hold for the field in problem 3?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                <m>\ds \mathbf F= \left(-\frac{y}{x^2+y^2},
    \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\right)</m>, so
                <md>
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial x}\left(F_2\right) = \frac{x^2+y^2 - 2x^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2}=\frac{-x^2+y^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2}
                </md>
                <md>    
                  \frac{\partial }{\partial y}\left(F_1\right) = \frac{(x^2+y^2)(-1) - (-y)(2y)}{(x^2+y^2)^2}=\frac{-x^2+y^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2}
                </md>
                
                Huh.  So the relationship holds, but it isn't a conservative field... So this relationship is not quite enough to prove 
                the field is conservative.  There seems to be one thing missing (we'll get there...)
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>